Even hard determinists must act as if they have free will — they deliberate about whether to accept determinism, implying the reasoning process matters, which is precisely what compatibilist free will claims.
Children develop moral responsibility gradually as their prefrontal cortex matures, and the law recognises this — we hold adults responsible in a way we do not hold infants, tracking the development of the very capacities free will requires.
When people act under coercion (a gun to their head) we rightly judge them differently than when they act freely — this distinction is coherent only if there is a meaningful sense in which free action exists.
Deliberating well — reasoning carefully, seeking information, testing conclusions — changes what you decide; this feedback loop between reasoning and action is precisely what free will requires.
If free will were impossible, the practice of giving and demanding reasons — the basis of all education, law, ethics, and personal relationships — would be unintelligible.
